13. FRONTEND ARCHITECTURE AND BACKEND INTEGRATION
The frontend must integrate with the existing backend architecture.
Existing infrastructure:
SharePoint Lists.
Power Automate Flows.
Existing APIs and configured endpoints.
The frontend is responsible for:
Capturing user information.
Displaying beneficiary records.
Submitting registration data.
Uploading supporting documents.
Retrieving submission details.
Displaying approval status.
Triggering existing approval actions.
Displaying notifications.
Managing users and schemes.
Do not:
Rebuild Power Automate workflows.
Implement new approval routing.
Generate independent approval statuses.
Create unnecessary beneficiary ID fields.
Replace SharePoint document storage.
Hardcode user records, schemes, or approval data.
Invent backend endpoints.
Use the existing reference number and employee information to identify submissions.
Integration requirements:
Create a centralised API service layer.
Keep endpoint URLs configurable.
Use environment variables for configuration.
Implement loading, success, empty, and error states.
Handle authentication and role-based access.
Ensure users only see records they are authorised to access.
Implement proper file upload handling.
Use backend-returned values as the source of truth.
Where endpoint details are unavailable, create clearly documented integration placeholders rather than inventing API contracts.
